\section{The spherical benchmark}\label{sec:S2}

We reimplemented the analytical model of \citet{jas2026}: a tangential current dipole of << F.g1a_q_nam >>~nAm in a spherical conductor (head radius << F.g1a_h_mm >>~mm, brain radius << F.g1a_b_mm >>~mm), with point magnetometers measuring the radial field on the scalp (OPM) and << F.g1a_xi_squid_mm >>~mm above it (SQUID). The single-channel signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is the peak field over the noise standard deviation $\sigma$, with $\sigma_{\mathrm{OPM}} = \eta\,\sigma_{\mathrm{SQUID}}$; the equal-SNR depth is the exact root of the preprint's Eq.~3. We checked the peak field against the full Sarvas field maximized over the sensor sphere (largest relative difference << F.g1a_eq1_vs_sarvas_rel_err_opm >>) and against the sphere model of MNE-Python \citep[<< F.g1a_eq1_vs_mne_rel_err_opm >>;][]{gramfort2013}.

The reimplementation reproduced the preprint (Fig.~\ref{fig:S1}A). At $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> the exact equal-SNR depth is << F.g1a_deq_eta3_mm_3dp >>~mm; the preprint prints << F.g1a_fig3_deq_printed_mm >>~mm and draws << F.g1a_fig3_deq_drawn_mm >>~mm, a difference of rounding and grid, not of model. A crossing exists only for $\eta$ between << F.g1a_eta0_exact >> and << F.g1a_eta1_exact >> (printed as << F.g1a_eta0_printed >> and << F.g1a_eta1_printed >>). Only $\eta$ enters the equal-SNR depth: scaling both noises together leaves it at << F.g1a_deq_sigma_independent_mm >>~mm.

On the real head, the ratio of the peak field at the best OPM to that at the best Neuromag magnetometer falls with depth as the sphere predicts once the sphere is given the real median standoffs (<< F.g2_matched_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm for the OPMs, << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm for the magnetometers). For the dense array it fell from << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_10_15_ratio >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm bin to << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_55_60_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm, against << F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_10_15_ratio >> and << F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_55_60_ratio >> for the sphere.

To bridge the two models we used the sphere's own metric, peak-channel SNR against the magnetometers with sensor noise only, a single-channel quantity rather than the primary endpoint. At $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> the realistic equal-SNR depth was << F.g2_bridge_deq_matched_eta3_mm >>~mm (site-matched array) and << F.g2_bridge_deq_dense_eta3_mm >>~mm (dense array), against << F.g2_bridge_deq_sphere_real_eta3_mm >>~mm for the sphere with the real standoffs. For $\eta$ from << F.g2_bridge_common_eta_min >> to << F.g2_bridge_common_eta_max >>, the arrays' depths lay within << F.g2_bridge_deq_arrays_minus_sphere_real_mm_range >>~mm of the sphere's (Fig.~\ref{fig:S1}B).

Outside that window the models differed. The OPMs were ahead at every depth for $\eta \le$ << F.g2_bridge_dense_eta_opm_ahead_max >>, where the sphere still predicts a deep crossing (<< F.g2_bridge_deq_sphere_real_eta2p25_mm >>~mm), because the real peak-field ratio levels off at depth (<< F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_60_65_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_60_65_mm >>~mm) while the sphere's keeps falling (<< F.g2_leadfield_sphere_real_60_65_ratio >>). Neuromag's magnetometers were ahead at every depth for $\eta \ge$ << F.g2_bridge_matched_eta_squid_ahead_min >> (site-matched) or << F.g2_bridge_dense_eta_squid_ahead_min >> (dense). The sphere is therefore right about the geometry of magnetometers near its reference $\eta$. Against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> planar gradiometers, the ratio with sensor noise only barely changed with depth, so the sphere describes only the magnetometer part of the comparison; $\eta$ itself is left to the explicit noise model (Section~\ref{sec:S3}).

For head size, we placed the preprint's newborn, << F.g3a_infant1yr_age_years >>-year, << F.g3a_child8yr_age_years >>-year and adult spheres in a SQUID shell that follows each head at << F.lit_xi_squid_mm >>~mm, as in the preprint, and, as a contrast, in the adult shell of radius << F.g3a_fixed_shell_radius >>~mm. With the size-following shell, the equal-SNR depth at $\eta$~=~<< F.lit_eta_reference >> hardly moved from adult to newborn (<< F.g3a_follow_eta3_deq_range >>~mm), but the normalized depth rose from << F.g3a_adult_follow_eta3_norm_1dp >> to << F.g3a_newborn_follow_eta3_norm_1dp >>, because the brain and the extracerebral layer (<< F.lit_table1_extracerebral_range_mm >>~mm in the preprint's heads) shrink. In the single adult shell, size and fit are confounded: the OPM was ahead at every depth of the newborn and << F.g3a_infant1yr_age_years >>-year brains up to $\eta$~=~<< F.g3a_newborn_fixed_eta0_1dp >> and << F.g3a_infant1yr_fixed_eta0_1dp >>, respectively. The sphere's prediction for children thus assumes a shell that follows the head at a constant gap.

